    <body>
      <sec id="article-27269.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>This continuing medical education activity provides a focused review of plague, a rare but potentially fatal zoonotic infection caused by <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis.</italic> Although plague is uncommon in many regions, delayed recognition and treatment can rapidly lead to sepsis, respiratory failure, meningitis, shock, and death. A key practice gap exists in clinicians' recognition of the varied clinical presentations of bubonic, septicemic, and pneumonic plague, particularly in patients with relevant travel, animal, flea, or endemic-area exposures. This activity is designed to narrow that gap by reviewing epidemiology, transmission, clinical manifestations, diagnostic strategies, infection-control precautions, antimicrobial treatment, recognition of complications, postexposure prophylaxis, and public health reporting. Participants are expected to gain improved diagnostic accuracy, earlier risk stratification, more timely initiation of evidence-based therapy, stronger prevention of complications and transmission, and more effective interdisciplinary coordination in the care of patients with suspected or confirmed plague.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify clinical and epidemiologic features that raise suspicion for plague in patients with compatible exposure histories, travel histories, or risk in endemic areas.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess patients with suspected plague using appropriate diagnostic testing, including culture, antigen-based testing, serologic evaluation, and molecular methods when available.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement timely empiric antimicrobial treatment for suspected plague based on clinical presentation, disease severity, and evidence-based therapeutic recommendations.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement interprofessional team strategies to improve care coordination and communication, advancing outcomes in cases of plague.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27269&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27269">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Plague is a zoonotic disease that has impacted humans for millennia. The main forms of plague in humans include bubonic, septicemic, and pneumonic plague, all caused by the gram-negative bacillus <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic>. The typical transmission cycle involves enzootic infection in rodent hosts, with fleas acting as vectors. The enzootic cycle is sometimes followed by transmission to nonrodent mammalian hosts, including humans, who serve as accidental hosts. Despite this, <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic> is arguably one of the most important microbes in human history. The bacterium has caused 3 documented pandemics, with the <italic toggle="yes">Black Death</italic> in 14th-century Europe leading to the death of up to 30% of the population. The most recent pandemic began in Asia, particularly India, in the late 19th century and spread worldwide; the bacterium now remains endemic in Africa, the Americas, and Asia.<italic toggle="yes"> Yersinia pestis</italic> also poses a potential bioterrorism threat, spurring ongoing vaccine development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>A single bacterium, <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic> of the family Yersiniaceae, causes the diverse clinical presentations of plague.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic> is a bipolar, gram-negative bacillus that grows well on most culture media (see <bold>Image. </bold>Gram Stain&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia enterocolitica</italic>). Bipolar staining may be more obvious with Wright-Giemsa or Wayson stains.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y pestis</italic>&#x000a0;originated&#x000a0;as a genetic&#x000a0;variant of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia</italic>&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">pseudo</italic><italic toggle="yes">tuberculosis</italic> (an enteric pathogen) after acquiring two pathogenic plasmids and enhancing the function of endogenous virulence factors several thousand years ago.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r5">[5]</xref> The bacterium is enzootic in rodent populations, with insects acting as the primary vectors. The most common hosts related to human transmission are <italic toggle="yes">Rattus rattus</italic>, the domestic black rat, and <italic toggle="yes">Rattus norvegicus</italic>, the brown sewer rat. The most efficient and common vector is <italic toggle="yes">Xenopsylla cheopis</italic>, the oriental rat flea, though many other flea species are also competent vectors. Humans typically acquire the bacterium through the bite of an infected flea, although several other routes of transmission are possible. Documented plague cases include transmission via direct handling of animal tissue with open skin lesions, inhalation of aerosolized bacteria, consumption of infected animals, and human-to-human spread.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Plague has had a profound impact on history, marked by 3 major pandemics. The first, known as the Justinianic Plague, swept across the Mediterranean in the 6th century during the reign of the Roman emperor Justinian. The second pandemic was the Black Death of the 14th century in Europe, while the third began in China in the late 19th century, leading to the deaths of millions in both China and India.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref> Over the first decade of the 21st century, 21,725 cases were reported with 1612 deaths, for a mortality rate of 7.4%, with the majority of cases occurring in&#x000a0; East and Central Africa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r6">[6]</xref> Since the mid-20th century, plague has been most active in Madagascar, with a total of 13,234 cases from 1998 to 2016. In the United States, 57 cases were documented between 2000 and 2009, resulting in 7 deaths. Overall, plague has been rare in the US throughout the 20th century, likely because of the sparsely populated nature of affected regions, including New Mexico, Colorado, California, and Texas.</p>
        <p>Regardless of location, plague spread is seasonal, driven by the ecology of rodents and their vectors in a given region. For example, plague season in the highlands of Madagascar stretches from October through February, but on the coast, cases occur mostly from August through October; while in the US, most cases occur between February and August.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref> All ages and both sexes are susceptible to the disease; however, recent data suggest a slight predominance among men and children, although the significance of this trend remains uncertain.</p>
        <p>The primary risk factor for plague is exposure to insect vectors and rodent hosts in endemic areas, with flea bites as the predominant mode of transmission. Bites, scratches, or respiratory secretions from infected cats can also transmit the disease, and person-to-person transmission is possible with pneumonic plague. Disease prevalence is higher in warm climates, especially for impoverished populations living in housing or shelters that are not rat-proof.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r1">[1]</xref> In nonendemic regions, clinicians should maintain a high index of suspicion for plague in immunocompromised individuals. Results from a recent case report described a fatal outcome in a patient with human immunodeficiency virus in Nigeria.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r7">[7]</xref> In the US, the risk of plague is highest in the southwestern states, with half of cases occurring in individuals aged 12 to 45 and more commonly affecting men and those engaged in outdoor activities. Suspected cases of plague must be reported to the appropriate local health department. In the United States, data on these cases are reported to local and state agencies and collected by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Although other means of transmission exist, <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic>&#x000a0;usually spreads from an enzootic rodent to a flea vector, where it forms a blockage in the flea's gastrointestinal tract (see <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Zoonotic Transmission Cycle of<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic><italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis).</italic>&#x000a0;This blockage causes the flea to feed more often and regurgitate <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic> into the host with each bite.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Upon entering the host,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y Pestis</italic>&#x000a0;has many mechanisms to evade the immune system and typically travels to the lymph nodes, where it replicates within macrophages. After release from macrophages, the bacterium causes a proinflammatory response that leads to clinical symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r10">[10]</xref> Lymph node involvement in the disease process causes 80% to 95% of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic> infections to present as suppurative adenitis, commonly known as bubonic plague.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r9">[9]</xref> However, this presentation can be bypassed in cases of primary infection of the bloodstream, causing septicemic plague, or the lungs, causing pneumonic plague. Septicemic plague presents with hypotension and shock without a bubo, whereas pneumonic plague presents with high fever, cough, chest pain, and hemoptysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The most common presentation of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic> infection in humans is bubonic plague; the disease begins with a flea bite and a 2- to 8-day incubation period. In 25% of patients, a skin lesion may be present at the bite site. This lesion presents differently in different patients, possibly as a pustule, vesicle, papule, or eschar, and is often not noticed. Patients then develop a sudden onset of fever, chills, headache, and general weakness. Within the next 24 hours, a bubo develops. The bubo begins as intense pain in a regional lymph node, most commonly the inguinal node, followed by the axillary or cervical nodes. Swelling then develops in the area of the bubo, and the pain may be so intense that patients avoid movement. A bubo may be singular or present as a cluster of nodes, but the masses are typically nonfluctuant, and overlying warmth is typically absent (see <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Suppurative Inguinal Lymphadenopathy (Bubo) Secondary to&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic><italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>Infection). In addition to fever, other vital sign abnormalities include tachycardia and hypotension progressing to shock. In addition to the bubo, the physical examination may demonstrate hepatosplenomegaly.</p>
        <p>Septicemic plague presents with high fever without an associated bubo and often rapidly progresses to sepsis. Additionally, pneumonic plague can occur as a primary or secondary infection. Secondary pneumonic plague most commonly occurs following hematogenous spread from the bubo and is characterized by cough, chest pain, and hemoptysis, in addition to the typical high fever associated with plague. Secondary pneumonic plague may occur with or without buboes. Primary inhalational pneumonic plague occurs following exposure to a coughing patient with pneumonic plague.</p>
        <p>Rare presentations include meningitis, which usually occurs secondary to untreated bubonic plague, and pharyngitis. This presentation is similar to other forms of pharyngitis and is characterized by significant inflammation of the anterior cervical lymph nodes. Plague may also present with prominent nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and abdominal pain before the presence of buboes or sepsis, making initial diagnosis difficult.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Patients with suspected plague should be isolated and treated until infection is excluded. For pneumonic plague, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommends standard and droplet precautions for 48 hours after initiation of appropriate antibiotic therapy. Laboratory personnel should be informed of suspected plague to ensure proper handling of samples, because plague isolates can be transmissible through standard sample and culture procedures.</p>
        <p>For all presentations of <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis&#x000a0;</italic>infection, a high index of clinical suspicion is required to make the diagnosis. Suspicion should be raised in patients with exposure to an endemic area with any of the following signs: fever after contact with rodents, unexplained adenitis with fever, septic shock, or gram-negative bacilli&#x000a0;found in a patient with pneumonia and hemoptysis. The presence of bipolar gram-negative bacilli in lymph node (bubo) aspirates, sputum, or cerebrospinal fluid can support a presumptive diagnosis in endemic areas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Complete blood counts may demonstrate significant leukocytosis (&#x0003e; 20,000/mm<sup>3</sup>)&#x000a0;combined with thrombocytopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;In areas of high disease prevalence, such as Madagascar, rapid testing using the&#x000a0;fraction 1 capsular antigen (F1) is common.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r10">[10]</xref> Tests with varying sensitivities and specificities are available, including immunofluorescence, polymerase chain reaction, and enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay. The specific test chosen depends on available resources and the disease prevalence in the practice setting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r12">[12]</xref> Definitive diagnosis is usually based on bacterial identification. In the United States, clinicians can identify isolates by sending cultures to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;In the absence of a bacterial isolate, the diagnosis can be made by seroconversion with a 4-fold or greater change in antibody titer to the F1 antigen. However, seroconversion&#x000a0;can take several weeks to occur after symptom onset.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27269.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="5" cellspacing="2" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 114.139px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Pause and Reflect</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 586.861px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A 42-year-old woman presents with a 3-day history of fever, chills, and severe pain in her left axilla. She lives in rural Colorado and reports finding a dead prairie dog near her home one week ago. Vital signs are temperature 39.5 &#x000b0;C (103.1&#x000b0;F) and blood pressure 90/55 mm Hg. Physical examination reveals a 5-cm tender, nonfluctuant mass in the left axilla. A bubo aspirate is obtained and sent for Gram stain and culture. What microscopic finding on the bubo aspirate would support a presumptive diagnosis of plague?&#x000a0;</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Early antibiotic therapy is the cornerstone of effective treatment for plague because of the rapid progression of each disease subtype. Given the potential for poor clinical outcomes, treatment should begin based on clinical suspicion. Aminoglycosides remain first-line therapy, with streptomycin historically preferred and gentamicin commonly used because of greater availability. Historically, courses&#x000a0;were administered for 10 days. However, 7-day courses also appear to be efficacious. Alternatives include doxycycline and tetracycline. Chloramphenicol is the preferred treatment for plague meningitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;In 2012, the US Food and Drug Administration approved levofloxacin for use in patients with plague based on animal and in vitro studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Experimental bacteriophage therapies have shown efficacy in animal models but are not approved for clinical use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>High clinical suspicion is essential for diagnosing plague, because the differential diagnosis for each presentation is quite broad. For bubonic plague, any presentation with lymphadenopathy in an ill-appearing patient should be included in the differential diagnosis; this includes a variety of bacterial, viral, fungal, oncologic, medication-related, autoimmune, and inflammatory causes of lymphadenopathy or lymphadenitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r15">[15]</xref> However, bubonic plague is unique in its presentation because of its sudden onset of fever and buboes, rapid progression of intense bubo inflammation, and a clinically fulminant course within days of symptom onset.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r11">[11]</xref> &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Similarly, the differential diagnosis for hemoptysis and fever is wide-ranging and includes viral bronchitis, pneumonia (particularly tuberculosis), pulmonary embolism, inflammatory disorders, and malignant neoplasm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r16">[16]</xref> However, the sudden onset and severe course of the disease, as well as the presence of buboes, can suggest plague over other etiologies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r11">[11]</xref> Septicemic plague may present more nonspecifically than other forms of plague, and its presentation of fever and hypotension is broad and encompasses all infectious and inflammatory causes of sepsis, as well as any cause of shock.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r17">[17]</xref> Clinical suspicion, followed by diagnostic testing, is particularly important in these patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r2">[2]</xref> Documented cases of travel-associated plague have occurred in the United States. Consequently, clinical suspicion should remain high in endemic areas and among patients with a history of recent travel to such areas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of all presentations of plague is poor if untreated and remains guarded even with treatment. Bubonic plague has an estimated mortality rate of 50% to 90% when untreated. Septicemic plague may be associated with a higher mortality rate. Proper diagnosis and early treatment of both etiologies reduces mortality to 5% to 15%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref> As noted above, mortality among known cases in the early 21st century is approximately 7%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r6">[6]</xref> Pneumonic plague mortality approaches 100% unless the disease is rapidly treated, and even with proper treatment, mortality is greater than 50%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Many potential complications of plague exist, ranging from death to various presentations other than classic bubonic plague. Significant complications include pharyngitis, secondary pneumonic plague, bacteremia, and septic shock. Additionally, meningitis is a known complication and typically occurs at least 1 week following untreated bubonic plague, leading to increased mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Even in plague-endemic regions, the unpredictable patterns of disease and outbreaks pose challenges for public health and for patients' recognition of disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r18">[18]</xref> Plague is even rarer in the US, and knowledge of the disease's risk factors and symptoms is not widespread.&#x000a0;Avoiding dead rodents is the most critical step in preventing infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r2">[2]</xref> For patients living in an endemic area, additional deterrence involves avoiding fleas and rodents by living in rat-proof houses, wearing shoes and clothing that cover the legs, and applying insecticides to the household as needed. Sick cats should be avoided, and dead animals should not be handled without gloves.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>If a person has concerns about exposure to pneumonic plague, they should receive postexposure chemoprophylaxis for 7 days. Doxycycline has been recommended for this indication, though other antibiotics have also been studied.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r19">[19]</xref> Postexposure chemoprophylaxis is also reasonable afte<bold>r</bold> a laboratory accident. In the event of a potential bioterrorism exposure, mass chemoprophylaxis may be considered. Vaccines to prevent&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Y pestis</italic>&#x000a0;infection exist, though none are in widespread use due to questions about&#x000a0;efficacy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;As of 2025, more than 20 candidate plague vaccines were in development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27269.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Plague is a zoonotic infection caused by <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic>, transmitted primarily through flea vectors from rodent reservoirs, with potential for direct contact or respiratory spread in pneumonic disease. The disease manifests in&#x000a0;3 principal forms: bubonic, characterized by painful regional lymphadenopathy (buboes) with acute fever; septicemic, presenting with rapidly progressive sepsis and shock without lymphadenopathy; and pneumonic, marked by fulminant pneumonia with hemoptysis and high transmissibility. Rapid progression and high mortality, particularly in untreated pneumonic plague, underscore the need for early clinical suspicion. Diagnosis is supported by exposure history, clinical findings, and laboratory identification of gram-negative bacilli, with confirmatory testing via culture or molecular methods. Immediate empiric antibiotic therapy with aminoglycosides or alternative agents is critical and should not be delayed pending confirmation, as early treatment significantly reduces mortality.</p>
        <p>Effective plague management requires coordination among clinicians, advanced practitioners, nurses, pharmacists, laboratory personnel, and public health officials, with each professional contributing specialized expertise to optimize patient outcomes and prevent disease transmission. Clinicians and advanced practitioners bear primary responsibility for maintaining a high level of clinical suspicion in endemic areas or among patients with relevant exposure histories, and for initiating empiric antibiotic therapy. Nurses play essential roles in implementing infection control measures, particularly standard and droplet precautions for patients with pneumonic plague, monitoring for clinical deterioration, and educating patients and families about transmission prevention. In addition to their usual roles, pharmacists in mass-exposure events coordinate dispensing of postexposure prophylaxis for exposed populations and assist in managing antimicrobial stockpiles.</p>
        <p>Laboratory personnel must be notified immediately of suspected plague cases to ensure proper biosafety measures during specimen handling, as <italic toggle="yes">Yersinia pestis</italic> isolates are transmissible through routine laboratory procedures. Laboratories facilitate rapid diagnosis through culture, polymerase chain reaction testing, and rapid antigen detection, with results communicated urgently to treating clinicians and public health authorities. Public health officials coordinate surveillance, contact tracing, and outbreak response activities that extend beyond individual patient care. Interprofessional communication must be direct, timely, and structured to facilitate rapid decision-making during plague treatment, both for individual patients and for potential population-level outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27269.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
